---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About" description="About Naman, what he likes to build, and why this personal website exists.">
  <div class="shell shell--content page-intro">
    <h1>A curious builder learning in public.</h1>
    <p>
      I’m Naman. I learn best by making something, breaking it a little, and writing down the useful part.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="shell shell--content about-grid">
    <article class="prose about-copy">
      <p>
        This site is where those experiments live. Sometimes they become a website or a tiny tool. Sometimes they turn into a note about the internet, technology, or the strange process of figuring things out as you go.
      </p>

      <h2 id="exploring">What I keep exploring</h2>
      <p>
        I’m interested in AI when it helps people make or understand something, SEO when it connects a useful page with the person looking for it, and websites that feel like they were made by an actual human. I also spend time around WordPress, small browser tools, and ideas that are still too early to have a neat label.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m not here to perform expertise. I’m here to document the work, the wrong turns, and the useful discoveries honestly.
      </p>

      <h2 id="off-screen">When I’m off-screen</h2>
      <p>
        Anime is a permanent part of the rotation, and <em>One Piece</em> is the favorite. I like the sense of adventure, the odd little crews people build around themselves, and the reminder that curiosity is a pretty good compass.
      </p>

      <h2>Why keep a personal site?</h2>
      <p>
        Social feeds are good at distributing fragments. A personal site is better at keeping the whole trail: projects beside essays, unfinished interests beside finished thoughts, and enough room for the design to carry a little personality.
      </p>

      <h2>Colophon</h2>
      <p>
        Naman.blog is built with <a href="https://astro.build/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>, styled with plain CSS and Tailwind’s build tooling, and hosted on <a href="https://pages.github.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Pages<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>. It intentionally uses no client-side framework and very little JavaScript.
      </p>
    </article>

    <aside class="about-aside" aria-label="A few coordinates">
      <h2>A few coordinates</h2>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Making</dt><dd>Websites, tools, experiments</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Learning</dt><dd>AI, SEO, better web craft</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Writing</dt><dd>Lessons, opinions, field notes</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Recharging</dt><dd>Anime, chai, long rabbit holes</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Layout>
